    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand you're looking for specifics, but it's important to emphasize that determining an \"upper limit\" for caffeine and green tea extract, especially when used together, is not just about milligrams or safe doses. The line between an effective metabolism booster and potentially harmful side effects can be quite thin and variable among individuals, particularly with high caffeine tolerance.\n\nWhile some reports suggest that green tea extract can contain up to 100 mg of L-theanine alongside catechins and caffeine, what matters more than just the milligrams is how your body reacts to these substances.\n\nCaffeine itself has a tolerance threshold which can be individual. The general consensus for short-term use, without considering long-term health impacts or your unique caffeine tolerance, suggests not exceeding 400 mg of caffeine per day. As a coffee-like substance, that's roughly equivalent to 4-6 cups of coffee, though green tea is technically much less potent.\n\nFor green tea extract, modest amounts, such as up to 200 mg per day for most people, are reported to be well-tolerated in supplement form. However, the combination of the two can yield much higher caffeine levels in your system that can abruptly increase heart rate and anxiety, and should not be done without professional guidance.\n\nI'd still",
